<p>
James Cherti has an interesting and useful post <a href="https://www.jamescherti.com/emacs-why-use-setq-instead-setopt/">about the relative efficiency of setq, setq-default, setopt, and customize-set-variable</a>. Cherti justifies this concern on their effect on Emacs startup time but according to well established Irreal dicta this doesn&rsquo;t matter because we seldom restart Emacs so a few seconds more or less simply don&rsquo;t matter.
</p>
<p>
Oddly, there are some people who pay no attention to Irreal dicta and who obsess about every millisecond—Cherti&rsquo;s term—of Emacs startup time. For those people and those who worry about execution times in general, Cherti&rsquo;s post is very useful and explains why some of those commands are faster than others.
</p>
<p>
The crux of the matter is that <code>defcustom</code> can include a setter method that can be arbitrarily complex. Cherti has a nice example of this that will help you understand what&rsquo;s going on. Cherti says that very often there&rsquo;s no reason to invoke that setter method <i>during startup</i> and that <code>setq</code> or <code>setq-default</code> is faster and just as useful. Commands like <code>setopt</code> and <code>customize-set-variable</code> always call the setter method so they may be slower. Cherti&rsquo;s examples show how to avoid unnecessary bottlenecks.
</p>
<p>
Cherti also shows how even the <code>:custom</code> keyword in <code>use-package</code> can trigger the setter methods of those variables and gives some workarounds. The main problem is that it&rsquo;s hard to know when you can avoid the setter method and when you can&rsquo;t. Sometimes, you just have to read the source code to figure it out.
</p>
<p>
Obsessing about startup times aside, it&rsquo;s nice to have an explanation of what these commands do and how their use might affect run time. Cherti&rsquo;s post is relatively short and well worth a minute or two to read.</p>
